---
/**
 * /weather/method — how the map is made.
 *
 * The figures in the prose are read from the current run rather than typed in,
 * so a changed grid or a changed land-cover coverage moves the sentence.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Legend from '../../components/weather/Legend.astro';
import { PAINT_FLOOR, currentRun, validationMethod } from '../../lib/weather';

const run = currentRun;
const w = run?.manifest.weather ?? {};
const t = run?.manifest.terrain ?? {};
const fresh = w.freshness ?? {};
const bandsAttached = run?.manifest.score_bands != null;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How the fruiting map is made | MycoSci"
  description="The fruiting-timeline model, its inputs, and why nothing on the map is green."
>
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-10">
    <div class="border-b border-lab-800 pb-8 mb-10">
      <a href="/weather" class="label text-bio-500 hover:text-bio-400">// Fruiting weather</a>
      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-semibold text-lab-100 mt-2 tracking-tight">
        How the map is made
      </h1>
      <p class="text-lab-400 mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        Weather in, colour out. There is no step in here where anybody looked at a forest and had
        an opinion about it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-invert prose-sm max-w-none prose-headings:font-semibold prose-headings:text-lab-100 prose-p:text-lab-400 prose-li:text-lab-400 prose-strong:text-lab-200 prose-a:text-bio-400 prose-a:no-underline hover:prose-a:text-bio-300">
      <h2>Fruiting is a timeline, not a forecast</h2>
      <p>
        The naive version of this map asks “is it wet and cool right now?” That is physically
        wrong. A mushroom is the last event in a sequence that started weeks earlier:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Wet-up.</strong> A soaking rain brings the soil to field capacity. This starts the
          clock. It is not the fruiting day.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Incubation.</strong> The mycelium grows underground for one to four weeks,
          depending on species, while the soil stays wet enough. If it dries out, the pins abort and
          nothing surfaces.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Fruiting.</strong> Sustained moisture plus the right temperature, and mushrooms
          appear — often on a dry, pleasant day, days after the rain that caused them.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        That last detail is why the naive model fails: the forager photographs the mushroom on the
        nice day, and a model reading that day's rainfall sees a dry afternoon and scores it zero.
      </p>

      <h2>What goes in</h2>
      <p>
        {w.grid_points ?? '—'} weather points across Oregon at {w.step_degrees ?? '—'}° spacing,
        each carrying about {fresh.max_days ?? '—'} days of hourly history from the{' '}
        {w.source ?? '—'} using the {w.model ?? '—'} model: temperature, humidity, precipitation,
        soil temperature, soil moisture at two depths, and reference evapotranspiration. Elevation
        comes from a USGS digital elevation model, land cover from NLCD, and the base map from
        OpenStreetMap.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each run verifies that the weather endpoint is still serving the model it claims before it
        renders anything. If that check fails, the run fails: the check hits the same endpoint the
        whole map is built from, so if it cannot run, the map cannot either.
      </p>

      <h2>What comes out</h2>
      <p>
        Every pixel gets a score between 0 and 1. A pixel is painted only if it clears{' '}
        {PAINT_FLOOR}; below that you are looking at bare base map. A pixel is <em>assessed</em>{' '}
        only where land cover is known — currently{' '}
        {(t.land_cover_coverage_pct ?? 0).toFixed(1)}% of the state — so the remainder is never
        judged at all and is left blank rather than guessed.
      </p>

      <h2>The colours, and the green problem</h2>
      <p>
        This map used to ramp from dark red through orange and yellow to green, with pure green as
        its top score. It was drawn over an OpenStreetMap base map on which every national forest is
        a solid pale green. Readers looked at Deschutes National Forest — scored flat zero, painted
        nothing at all — saw a green blob, and called it a hotspot. The map's brightest colour and
        its commonest background colour were the same hue.
      </p>
      <p>
        The ramp now runs lavender → blue-purple → magenta → orange → yellow and contains no green
        anywhere, and neither does this section.{' '}
        <strong>Nothing green on any image here came from the model.</strong>
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="my-8">
      <Legend
        caption="The ramp is stretched at the low end — positions are the square root of the score — because the interesting range of a fruiting score is bunched down there. The legend printed on each image is drawn by the same function, so you can hold one against the other."
      />
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-invert prose-sm max-w-none prose-headings:font-semibold prose-headings:text-lab-100 prose-p:text-lab-400 prose-li:text-lab-400 prose-strong:text-lab-200 prose-a:text-bio-400 prose-a:no-underline hover:prose-a:text-bio-300">
      <h2>How it is checked</h2>
      <p>
        Scores are compared against real occurrence records from iNaturalist and GBIF, and against
        <em> effort-matched controls</em>: places where somebody recorded a different fungus in the
        same season. That control set is the important part. Comparing records against random Oregon
        coordinates would mostly measure where people go, not where mushrooms are, and the model
        would score well by learning the road network.
      </p>
      {validationMethod && <p class="!text-lab-500 !text-xs">{validationMethod}</p>}
      <p>
        The result is an <strong>enrichment ratio</strong> — how much more likely a real record is
        to sit on given ground than an effort-matched control is. It is a comparison, not a chance.{' '}
        <a href="/weather/limits">Why it can never be a percentage</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Bands</h2>
      <p>
        Where the measurement resolves, it produces cut points, and the run can carry them in its
        manifest. Where it does not, the renderer emits{' '}
        <code class="font-mono text-bio-400">score_bands: null</code> and the map ships with a raw
        ramp and no band names at all. This section follows that literally: with no bands in the
        manifest, no band name appears anywhere near a map, because a name for a band nobody
        measured is a guess wearing a label.
      </p>
      <p>The current run {bandsAttached ? 'carries calibrated bands.' : 'carries none.'}</p>

      <h2>How it reaches this page</h2>
      <p>
        The pipeline writes a run directory containing the rendered PNGs and a{' '}
        <code class="font-mono text-bio-400">manifest.json</code> — score distributions, land-cover
        coverage, weather provenance, code revision, a SHA-256 per image, and a{' '}
        <code class="font-mono text-bio-400">publishable</code> flag. A publish step verifies each
        image against its manifest digest, converts it to a 256-colour PNG for delivery, ships it to
        the map origin, and commits the numbers here. A run marked{' '}
        <code class="font-mono text-bio-400">publishable: false</code> is refused outright: the
        pipeline exits non-zero rather than emit a degraded map, and this site does not paper over
        that by showing an older run under a newer date.
      </p>
      <p>
        The images are served from a host we run, addressed by run date and image digest. That means
        an origin which has not yet received today's render can only fail to load — it can never
        serve yesterday's map under today's date.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
